package cosmonic:agent@0.5.0;

/// Types shared by the spawn and tool interfaces added in this version of
/// `cosmonic:agent`. Not published; for discussion. See README.md and
/// DESIGN-NOTES.md. The version is the next one after 0.4.0 (PR #71, with the
/// tools fold and the `run` export); this file depends on 0.4.0 for messages,
/// tool types, and the records a run ends with (`run-types`).
///
/// A spawned child is driven through its `cosmonic:agent/run@0.4.0` export,
/// the same interface a host drives a top-level agent through. The parent
/// never sees that export's rich events. It sees the narrowed view here:
/// contentless `progress`, its own `start-error` and `run-error`, and the
/// shared `run-outcome`. The implementer calls the child's export, mints the
/// child's run id, turns the budget into per-run options, discards deltas and
/// steps, replaces the child's error details with its own, and buffers events
/// so a parent is never blocked.
///
/// Importing this interface grants nothing. The powers live in `spawn`,
/// `tool-discovery` and `tool-loading`, each a separate import so a world
/// grants exactly the ones it names.
///
/// The contract below binds the implementer of those interfaces, whatever it
/// is: a native host plugin, a host-installed policy layer in front of it, or
/// a test double. "The caller" is the component the import is bound to.
///
/// Caller identity. One identity is used everywhere these interfaces bind a
/// caller: in the approval digest, in coalescing, in the pending-approval
/// cap and denial cooldown, and in scoping loaded toolsets. It is the pair
/// of two host-assigned values:
///
/// - the workload incarnation: an id the host mints when the workload is
///   created and keeps for that workload's whole life, across rollouts to
///   new revisions (as `tool-loading.load` does) and instance restarts. A
///   workload deleted and recreated under the same name gets a new
///   incarnation, so it is a different caller.
/// - the component name: the caller's name within its workload, as the
///   workload definition gives it. It is stable across revisions and
///   restarts, and it tells sibling components of one workload apart.
///
/// Never an instance id: a rollout replaces every instance, so an instance
/// id would make a caller a stranger to its own pending approvals and loaded
/// toolsets. Workload namespace and name are display and audit metadata, not
/// identity. A policy wrapper must be installed by the host and pass the
/// original caller through: a guest component in front of the implementer
/// would become the caller, and its own grants would become the ceiling.
///
/// Reach. A request's read reach is the union, over the agent and every
/// toolset, of its read grants: volumes, secrets, interfaces the implementer
/// classifies as reading, and the model alias. Its egress reach is the
/// union of allowed hosts and interfaces the implementer classifies as
/// writing. A read-write volume counts both ways.
///
/// Ceiling. By default a child's read reach and egress reach, or a loaded
/// toolset's, must each fit inside the caller's. Within that ceiling, results
/// flowing up are not a leak: the caller could have read the same data
/// itself. Containment is decided on what a grant reaches, not on its name:
/// a volume fits only if it is backed by the same storage as one of the
/// caller's, and an allowed host fits only if one of the caller's entries
/// contains it (`*.github.com` contains `api.github.com`, not `github.com`).
/// A grant whose reach the implementer cannot see or compare is beyond the
/// ceiling. Volume names are never compared: two workloads may use one name
/// for different storage, so an implementer that cannot see the storage
/// behind a caller's mount treats every volume grant from that caller as
/// beyond the ceiling.
///
/// Attenuation. A caller may request more than the ceiling but cannot grant
/// it. The implementer is the sole grantor, by policy or auditor agent or
/// human in the loop, and returns `needs-approval`. An approval is an
/// explicit declassification (a decision that data may cross a boundary it
/// otherwise could not), bound to a digest over the caller's identity and the
/// whole request. Content the caller sends down can reach the child's egress;
/// an approval of egress beyond the ceiling says so too.
///
/// Authorship. A model can paraphrase anything it read, so redaction is not
/// a security boundary and a child's final reply is a declassification
/// channel by nature. Nothing else the caller sees carries child-authored
/// text: progress, errors, and reasons are implementer-authored, and every
/// detail string is diagnostic only, not stable, and not for branching on.
/// The child's journal is never observable through these interfaces.
///
/// Toolsets. A toolset is one component, with its own grants, that exports
/// `cosmonic:agent/tools@0.4.0` (the `tool-provider` world): the same
/// interface an agent imports. Its `call` rejects a tool name it does not own
/// with `not-dispatched(unknown-tool)` before doing any work. It egresses as
/// itself: a call is host-internal, made with the toolset's own grants and
/// component identity, even though the toolset is a component in the
/// agent's own workload; the calling agent and its session are audit context,
/// never delegated authority. The implementer may offer a tool under an alias
/// that is not its own name (`<toolset>__<tool>` when another toolset already
/// has the name) and passes the tool's own name to `call`, so a description
/// should not name sibling tools. The implementer may bound what a call
/// returns before the agent sees it: text beyond its cap is cut at a UTF-8
/// boundary and followed by a text part of the form `[output truncated:
/// showing N of M bytes; narrow the request]`; a structured result beyond the
/// cap becomes `is-error` with a text part saying it was too large; an inline
/// attachment beyond its own cap is replaced by a text note.
///
/// Steerable channels. The child cannot author text the caller sees outside
/// its reply, but it does choose its counts, its finish code, which error
/// kind a run ends in, whether an outcome exceeds `output.max-bytes`, and
/// its timing. Each of these is a narrow channel to the caller, minor next
/// to the reply itself. No interface here closes covert or timing channels.
///
/// Credentials never pass through these interfaces. Models, secrets and
/// credential references are named; the implementer resolves them and keeps
/// values out of every agent's memory. A spawned agent's own component never
/// receives secrets.
///
/// Powers. A child never inherits its caller's powers. It gets `spawn` only
/// when its request asks for it (`spawn-request.powers`) and the implementer
/// has spawn enabled; discovery and loading are never granted to a spawned
/// child in this version.
///
/// Authority to decide. A spawned agent never holds approval or release
/// authority: the grantor is a person, or policy a person wrote. An auditor
/// in the policy stack is installed by host policy, never spawned, so the
/// agent it reviews cannot choose its grants, budget or lifetime.
///
/// # References
/// - OCI image digests: https://github.com/opencontainers/image-spec/blob/main/descriptor.md#digests
/// - JSON Schema: https://json-schema.org/specification
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn-types {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use cosmonic:agent/inference-types@0.4.0.{json};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use cosmonic:agent/run-types@0.4.0.{run-outcome, run-counts};

    /// A component image pinned by content digest, so a moving tag cannot
    /// change what was approved.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record component-ref {
        /// An OCI reference such as `ghcr.io/acme/agents/coder:1.2.0`. For
        /// display; `digest` is authoritative.
        image: string,
        /// An OCI digest, `sha256:<hex>`.
        digest: string,
    }

    /// A named volume mounted into one component. Read-write counts toward
    /// both read and egress reach.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record volume-mount {
        /// The name of a volume the implementer knows, resolved in the
        /// caller's scope. Never a host path. The implementer compares the
        /// storage behind the name with the storage behind the caller's
        /// mounts; two workloads may use the same name for different storage.
        volume: string,
        /// Where the component sees the volume.
        mount-path: string,
        /// True mounts the volume read-only.
        read-only: bool,
    }

    /// An interface granted to one component. The implementer, not the
    /// request, classifies it as reading, writing, or both.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record interface-grant {
        /// Fully qualified interface name with version, such as
        /// `wasi:keyvalue/store@0.2.0`.
        name: string,
        /// Interface-specific configuration, such as a bucket or topic.
        config: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// What one component may touch. Empty grants nothing; nothing is
    /// inherited from the caller.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record grants {
        /// Hosts this component may reach.
        allowed-hosts: list<string>,
        /// Volumes mounted into this component.
        volumes: list<volume-mount>,
        /// Interfaces this component may import.
        interfaces: list<interface-grant>,
        /// Secret names the implementer delivers into this component's
        /// memory. Names only, never values. Refused on a spawned agent's own
        /// component (`spawn-request.grants`), whose memory credentials never
        /// enter; use `credentials` for authenticated egress.
        secrets: list<string>,
        /// Credential references: stable logical names, not secret data. The
        /// implementer resolves each through a credential binding the
        /// operator scoped to this component's workload deployment, applies
        /// it to the component's outbound requests (an injected Authorization
        /// header, an mTLS client identity, or a signed request) and owns
        /// those authentication headers, so a value the component supplies for
        /// one is rejected. The value never enters the component. An
        /// implementer without a credential broker treats every reference as
        /// beyond the ceiling.
        credentials: list<string>,
    }

    /// A toolset: one component, with its own grants, that offers one or more
    /// tool definitions. Used by `spawn` for a child's toolsets and by
    /// `tool-loading` for a toolset the caller loads. "Tool" means a single
    /// definition or call, as in `cosmonic:agent/tools`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record toolset-spec {
        /// A DNS label: lowercase letters, digits and `-`, starting and
        /// ending with a letter or digit, at most 32 characters. Unique among
        /// the toolsets of one agent. Display metadata and the prefix of a
        /// namespaced alias; never used for routing or authorization, which
        /// follow the toolset's component identity and digest.
        name: string,
        /// A component targeting `cosmonic:agent/tool-provider@0.4.0`: it
        /// exports `tools`. An implementer may also accept a component
        /// exporting `cosmonic:agent/tools@0.3.0`, whose `list` cannot fail.
        /// The implementer lists and dispatches across the toolset components
        /// in the agent's workload, so an agent never carries a router of its
        /// own.
        component: component-ref,
        /// What this toolset component may touch.
        grants: grants,
        /// The operator's override of how this toolset's tools are offered.
        /// None takes the default: each tool keeps its own name if no alias in
        /// the session has it yet, and otherwise is offered as
        /// `<name>__<tool>`. Some(prefix) offers every tool as the prefix
        /// followed by the tool's own name, so the empty string means the
        /// tools' own names and `"gh__"` means `gh__search`. An alias that is
        /// already taken, or that breaks model tool-name rules (only
        /// `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, at most 64 characters), refuses the spawn or load
        /// with `invalid-request`; the implementer never renames it.
        /// Operator-owned: an operator sets it in a toolset declaration. In a
        /// caller's spawn or load request an implementer may refuse it, and
        /// does unless its operator lets callers choose prefixes.
        alias-prefix: option<string>,
    }

    /// What may come back to the caller.
    /// Covered by the approval digest.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record output-spec {
        /// JSON Schema that `structured` must satisfy. None means reply
        /// parts only.
        schema: option<json>,
        /// Ceiling on the encoded outcome of one run, in bytes.
        max-bytes: u32,
    }

    /// What the child may spend. Covered by the approval digest. None takes
    /// the implementer's default, which policy may cap. ("Limit" is kept for
    /// the implementer's own caps: `limit-scope` and `over-limit`.)
    ///
    /// The implementer enforces the budget, not the child: it refuses a run
    /// past `max-runs`, ends a run at `max-duration`, and stops accepting runs
    /// once `max-total-tokens` is spent. Counts the child reports about
    /// itself (steps, token usage when the implementer does not meter model
    /// calls) are steerable by the child, so enforcement that relies on them
    /// is only as good as the child's honesty.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record budget {
        /// Runs the child may take: the number of `agent.run` calls
        /// accepted.
        max-runs: option<u32>,
        /// Model calls the child may make in one run. This is the bound
        /// that stops a runaway run. The OpenAI Agents SDK, the Claude Agent
        /// SDK and Goose call this bound `max_turns`. The implementer passes
        /// it to the agent in `run-options`, where it is advisory: the agent
        /// counts its own steps. The implementer enforces it only where it
        /// meters the child's model calls itself.
        max-steps: option<u32>,
        /// Tokens the child may spend across all runs and all model calls:
        /// the sum of `usage.prompt-tokens` and `usage.completion-tokens`.
        /// `cached-prompt-tokens`, `cache-write-tokens` and
        /// `reasoning-tokens` are parts of those two and are not added
        /// again. Not `chat-options.max-tokens`, which bounds the
        /// tokens one call generates. An implementer that does not meter the
        /// child's model calls as they happen enforces this when a run ends,
        /// so the run that crosses it may overshoot.
        max-total-tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock budget, measured from spawn. Passing it mid-run ends
        /// the run with an outcome whose finish is `max-duration`, not an
        /// error.
        max-duration: option<duration>,
    }

    /// Powers a spawned agent may receive. Discovery and loading are never
    /// granted to a spawned child in this version.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    flags agent-powers {
        /// The child may call `spawn`.
        spawn,
    }

    /// Everything needed to start a sub-agent. Declarative: its toolsets are
    /// fixed for the agent's lifetime. Construction is lenient; `spawn`
    /// validates and returns `invalid-request`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawn-request {
        /// A component targeting `cosmonic:agent/runnable-agent@0.4.0` (it
        /// exports `run`), so the implementer can hand it runs in process.
        agent: component-ref,
        /// A model alias, as `models.open` takes it. Resolved against the
        /// aliases the caller's spawn grant delegates, since a caller that
        /// never chats has no aliases of its own. Counts toward read reach
        /// like any other grant.
        model-alias: string,
        /// The child's instructions. Caller free text: not covered by the
        /// approval digest and never shown in its summary (see
        /// `approval.request-digest`).
        instructions: string,
        /// What the agent component itself may touch, in the same shape as a
        /// toolset's grants. Toolsets carry their own.
        grants: grants,
        /// The child's toolsets. Names must be unique.
        toolsets: list<toolset-spec>,
        /// What may come back, and how much.
        output: output-spec,
        /// What the child may spend over its lifetime.
        budget: budget,
        /// Powers the child receives. Empty grants none: a child never
        /// inherits its caller's powers. `spawn` is admitted only when the
        /// implementer has spawn enabled for the caller; it lets the child
        /// spawn in turn, within any operator limits.
        powers: agent-powers,
        /// For logs, audit, and display. Never policy input, and not
        /// covered by the approval digest.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// A grantor's decision on a request that needed approval.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant decision {
        /// Granted. Resend the identical request; the grant admits one
        /// request in total, however many waiters shared the approval.
        granted,
        /// Denied, with an implementer-authored reason.
        denied(string),
        /// No decision before the approval expired.
        expired,
        /// A decision not captured above. Treat as denied.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// A request beyond the caller's ceiling, sent to a grantor. The
    /// approval outlives the call that produced it: it stays pending until
    /// the grantor decides or it expires, whether or not anyone is waiting.
    /// It is bound to the request digest. A changed request, or the same
    /// request from another caller, is a new request. Standing permission
    /// belongs in policy a person wrote, not in a reusable approval.
    ///
    /// One logical request, one admission. Every identical request from the
    /// same caller made while an approval is pending is the same logical
    /// request: it returns that same pending approval (the same `id`, one
    /// prompt, not many), and every waiter's `decision` resolves to the same
    /// value. A grant is single use and is consumed atomically, exactly
    /// once, across all of those waiters and every resend: the implementer
    /// admits one request on it (one child started, or one rollout) and no
    /// more, and serializes concurrent resends so exactly one is admitted.
    /// The implementer cannot tell waiters with the same digest apart, so
    /// they share that one admission, and the caller hands the one `agent`
    /// handle or `loaded-toolset` to whichever of its waiters need it.
    /// Coalescing saves the person prompts; it never multiplies what one
    /// decision admits.
    ///
    /// A grant expires too: it admits the identical request only within the
    /// implementer's grant lifetime, set by the operator. Once a grant is
    /// consumed, or expires unused, an identical request is a new,
    /// independent request and needs its own approval: it returns
    /// `needs-approval` with a new approval, never the consumed one, and
    /// identical requests coalesce onto that new approval while it is
    /// pending. This includes a resend that lost the race for the grant.
    /// The implementer may cap how many approvals one caller has pending, so
    /// a looping agent cannot bury the person in prompts (it gets
    /// `over-limit`). "The same caller" means the same caller identity (see
    /// the contract on this interface): the same workload incarnation and
    /// the same component name. A workload recreated under the same name is a
    /// different caller and needs its own approval; a rollout to a new
    /// revision (as `load` does) keeps the caller. A sibling component in the
    /// same workload is a different caller: its identical request neither
    /// coalesces onto this approval nor consumes its grant. Only a person, or
    /// policy a person wrote, decides; never a spawned agent.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record approval {
        /// Implementer-issued id, to correlate with the approval UI and audit log.
        id: string,
        /// `sha256:<hex>` over a domain tag naming the operation and its
        /// version (`cosmonic:agent/spawn-request@2` or
        /// `cosmonic:agent/toolset-spec@2`), a zero byte, the caller's
        /// workload incarnation, a zero byte, the caller's component name, a
        /// zero byte, and the digest input in canonical JSON. Compare for
        /// equality only.
        ///
        /// What it covers: exactly the fields `summary` shows, and all of
        /// them. For a load, the whole `toolset-spec`. For a spawn, every
        /// `spawn-request` field except `instructions` and `labels`: those
        /// are caller free text, which the summary never shows, so the person
        /// never decides on them, and a digest over them would let a caller
        /// raise prompts that differ in nothing the person can see.
        /// Instructions are content sent down, like `agent.run` messages.
        ///
        /// Normalization, before digesting and before the ceiling check:
        /// - `allowed-hosts` entries are parsed; the scheme and host are
        ///   lowercased, an internationalized host becomes its A-label form,
        ///   one trailing dot is stripped, and a port equal to the scheme's
        ///   default (80 for `http` and `ws`, 443 for `https` and `wss`) is
        ///   dropped. A port with no scheme is kept. An entry that does not
        ///   parse is `invalid-request`.
        /// - Lists that are sets (`allowed-hosts`, `secrets`, `credentials`,
        ///   `volumes`, `interfaces`) drop exact duplicates and are sorted
        ///   by the UTF-8 bytes of each element's canonical JSON. Two
        ///   different elements with one key (two volumes at one
        ///   `mount-path`, two interface grants with one `name`) are
        ///   `invalid-request`. `toolsets` is ordered and keeps its order,
        ///   which assigns aliases.
        /// - Names, digests, paths and secret and credential names are
        ///   compared as given; no case folding or Unicode normalization.
        ///
        /// Encoding of each WIT value as JSON, then serialized by RFC 8785
        /// (JCS: sorted member names, no insignificant whitespace):
        /// - record: an object with every field, named as in this WIT
        ///   (kebab-case).
        /// - string: a JSON string. bool: `true` or `false`. u32: a number.
        /// - u64 and `duration`: a string of decimal digits with no leading
        ///   zeros, since RFC 8785 numbers are IEEE 754 doubles.
        /// - option: `null` for none, the value's encoding for some.
        /// - list: an array. list<tuple<string, string>> (interface
        ///   `config`): an object; a repeated key is `invalid-request`.
        /// - flags: an array of the names of the set flags, sorted.
        /// - variant: the case name as a string for a case with no payload,
        ///   otherwise an object with one member, the case name, whose value
        ///   is the payload's encoding.
        /// - `json` (`output.schema`): parsed and embedded as a JSON value;
        ///   invalid JSON or a repeated member name is `invalid-request`.
        request-digest: string,
        /// Implementer-authored text for the person who decides, like
        /// `toolset-offer.summary`, and unlike diagnostic detail strings.
        /// States what is declassified, for example "results of this agent
        /// (schema S, at most N bytes) return to <caller>". Built only from
        /// values the implementer has validated (parsed hosts, checked names
        /// and digests); free text the caller wrote, such as instructions or
        /// labels, never appears in it. It shows every input of
        /// `request-digest`, in normalized form, so two approvals whose
        /// digests differ never read the same; a caller-authored value with
        /// no safe rendering (`output.schema`) is shown by the `sha256:` of
        /// its canonical JSON.
        summary: string,
        /// Resolves when the grantor decides. Dropping it stops waiting; it
        /// does not withdraw the request, so a person who decides after the
        /// caller's own request has ended is not too late.
        decision: future<decision>,
    }

    /// Which implementer limit a request would exceed. The tree limits
    /// (`concurrent-agents`, `depth`, `total-agents`) are operator limits:
    /// optional, set in host or policy configuration, and unset by default,
    /// so nothing caps them unless an operator chooses to. A child's budget
    /// is chosen by its caller and is not carved from the caller's own.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant limit-scope {
        /// Agents running at once for this caller. May clear when one ends.
        concurrent-agents,
        /// Nesting depth of spawned agents, where a child of a workload the
        /// person started is at depth 1. Does not clear.
        depth,
        /// Agents spawned in total by one caller tree: the root caller and
        /// every descendant. Ended agents still count. Does not clear.
        total-agents,
        /// Memory the implementer reserves for the child.
        memory,
        /// Toolsets loaded into one agent at once. May clear when one is
        /// unloaded.
        loaded-toolsets,
        /// A scope not captured above.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// An implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record over-limit {
        /// Which limit.
        scope: limit-scope,
        /// The limit's value, where the implementer reports it.
        limit: option<u64>,
    }

    /// Why a spawn did not start an agent, or a load did not load a toolset.
    /// Either way nothing ran: no child started and no toolset was attached.
    /// Detail strings are implementer-authored and diagnostic only.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant admission-error {
        /// The operator has not enabled this power on this host, or did not
        /// grant it to this agent. Permanent for this caller: do not retry,
        /// adapt instead (for example, hide a delegate action).
        disabled,
        /// A policy or a person refused. The capability is partially granted:
        /// a different request may succeed.
        access-denied(string),
        /// Beyond the ceiling and sent to a grantor. Await `decision`, then
        /// resend the identical request. A grant admits one request in
        /// total; see `approval`.
        needs-approval(approval),
        /// An implementer limit would be exceeded.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// Malformed: a malformed digest, a duplicate toolset name, an unknown
        /// model alias, a component that does not target the right world.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// The implementer could not serve the call. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    // The records a run ends with (`run-outcome`, `run-counts`, `finish`,
    // `reply-part`, `served-model`) are the shared ones in
    // `cosmonic:agent/run-types@0.4.0`. In a spawned child's outcome, the
    // implementer authors `finish` from its budget (`max-steps` is
    // `budget.max-steps`, `max-total-tokens` and `max-duration` the
    // lifetime budget) and fills `model` from its own metering, and every
    // count it does not meter is only as honest as the child.

    /// Why a run is paused. Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant blocked-reason {
        /// A tool call waits on a person or the auditor.
        tool-approval,
        /// Policy holds the outcome until a person or the auditor releases it.
        outcome-release,
        /// A reason not captured above.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// Contentless progress. The implementer coalesces frames and never
    /// blocks on an unread stream. `counts` frames are cumulative but may lag:
    /// model calls an implementer does not meter appear only when the run
    /// ends.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant progress {
        /// The child is working.
        working,
        /// The run is paused.
        blocked(blocked-reason),
        /// Cumulative counters for this run so far.
        counts(run-counts),
        /// A frame not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// A budget item that can leave an agent unable to take another run.
    /// Per-run bounds such as `max-steps` end a run instead; see `finish`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant budget-item {
        /// `budget.max-runs` runs were accepted.
        max-runs,
        /// `budget.max-total-tokens` were spent across all runs.
        max-total-tokens,
        /// `budget.max-duration` passed.
        max-duration,
        /// An item not captured above.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// Why a run did not start. In every case nothing reached the agent.
    /// Returned by `agent.run`. Not the `run` export's own `start-error`:
    /// this one adds what only an implementer that holds a budget can know
    /// (`exhausted`, `failed`), and never says `duplicate`, since the
    /// implementer mints the child's run ids.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant start-error {
        /// A run is already in progress. One run at a time.
        busy,
        /// A budget item is spent; the agent takes no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The message or options cannot be taken, for example an attachment
        /// the agent does not accept. A different message may succeed.
        invalid-input(string),
        /// The agent has ended for good (it failed to start, or stopped and
        /// cannot be restarted) and will never accept another run. Details
        /// are on the observation path, not here.
        failed(string),
        /// The run could not be accepted now. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// Why a run failed after it started. Detail strings are
    /// implementer-authored, never the child's, and diagnostic only. Not the
    /// `run` export's own `run-error`: the child's typed cases (model errors,
    /// `not-recorded`, `superseded`) are child-authored and reach the parent
    /// only as `failed`, and `withheld` and `unavailable` are the
    /// implementer's.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-error {
        /// The caller dropped the progress stream or the agent. Cancellation
        /// is cooperative: effects the child or its toolsets caused before they
        /// stopped are not rolled back.
        cancelled,
        /// The outcome failed `output.schema` or exceeded `output.max-bytes`.
        output-rejected(string),
        /// A person or the auditor declined to release the outcome.
        withheld,
        /// The child failed. Details are on the observation path, not here.
        failed(string),
        /// The implementer could not finish serving the run.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent.
        other(option<string>),
    }

    /// A run that has started: progress frames, then the outcome. Not the
    /// `run` export's `started-run`, whose stream carries the child's text and
    /// steps. The stream closes before the outcome resolves; a closed stream alone does not mean success. The
    /// outcome can be awaited without draining the stream. Dropping the
    /// stream requests cancellation; an outcome already recorded wins the
    /// race, so a cancelled run may still resolve to its outcome.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    type started-run = tuple<stream<progress>, future<result<run-outcome, run-error>>>;
}
